# Code comments (implementation)

When you **add or materially change** application code, include comments that help the next reader. Match the tone and density of neighboring files in the same module.

## Do comment

- **Why** a non-obvious choice was made (sync conflict handling, tenancy checks, offline outbox ordering).
- **Protocols and invariants** (revision rules, idempotency, server-wins cases) — point to ADR when applicable.
- **Multi-step flows** — brief block comment above sync push/pull, migration upgrade, or auth pipeline sections.
- **Public module boundaries** — one-line docstring on services and non-trivial exported functions (Python `"""..."""`; TS on exported functions/types when behavior isn’t obvious from names).

## Do not comment

- Obvious getters, simple CRUD wiring, or code that duplicates the name (`// set name to name`).
- Every line inside a loop or standard React render boilerplate.
- Narrating syntax the language already expresses.

## Balance

- Prefer **fewer, higher-value** comments over noise.
- If logic is hard to explain in one short comment, **simplify the code** or document in `docs/adr/` and link from a single comment.
- Do not comment instead of tests for behavior; comments explain intent, tests prove behavior.

## Language notes

- **Python:** Module docstring optional for tiny files; service methods that encode business rules should have a one-line docstring or a short comment above the tricky branch.
- **TypeScript:** Use `//` or block comments sparingly in `sync/`, `db/`, and domain hooks; UI components only where UX/state logic is non-obvious.
